  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nHighest chances for rain come Thursday night into Friday morning:\n\nThe stationary front will finally get some movement Thursday\nnight as a rather potent mid-level wave for late July drops\nstraight south along the Missouri River in Nebraska, forcing a\nsurface low within Nebraska/Kansas. This will advance the\nstationary boundary eastward as a warm front as a warm air\nadvection response develops. The warm air and moisture advection\nover the front occurs, a thunderstorm complex is forecast to \ndevelop in the northern and central Plains, dropping southeast \ninto the area late Thursday night into Friday morning (40-80% \nchance). HREF and REFS members suggest that as the complex makes\nit into our area, MUCAPE/Shear values will dwindle, leading to \nthe activity to be rather messy with lightning and heavy rain \nbeing the main hazards. That said, a few strong wind gusts \ncannot be ruled out in any stronger storm.\n\n\nMarginal Risk for severe storms Friday afternoon and evening:\n\nThe complex is forecast to weaken as it moves west to east\nacross the area Friday morning. This provides some uncertainty\nin how widespread cloud debris is and how long that hangs around\nFriday, which would impact storm chances that Friday afternoon.\nPutting that uncertainty aside for now, the setup has the\nanomalous wave and associated surface low and cold front moving\nthrough Friday afternoon. Instability will be no problem, and\nshear should increase ahead of the wave, which will bring a\nMarginal (1 of 5) Risk for severe weather. It's a bit too early\nto say anything definitive as synoptic conditions often change\ndrastically 3 days out in late Summer. As a representation of\nthis, there is still additional uncertainty in the magnitude of\nshear with 10th-90th percentile LREF members ranging from 20-35\nkts. So if solutions trend toward the greater shear values, \nwith the low triple point forecast to move through central \nMissouri and a narrow warm sector pushing through our area ahead\nof the strong front, an all hazard threat could develop. \nContinue to monitor the forecast for any potential upgrades in \nthe severe risk.\n\n\nUnseasonably cool temperatures and windy conditions this weekend:\n\nEFI and ESAT ensemble forecasts point to this system being\nrather anomalous (dynamic) for late summer. A relatively strong\ncold front will surge through with the wave. This will bring\nunseasonably cool temperatures with highs in the middle 70s to\nmiddle 80s Saturday and Sunday. It will also bring unseasonably\nwindy conditions Saturday. The EFI says approximately 80-90% of\nmodels are above the climatologically normal distribution with\nsome members on the extreme side. This is a result of a\nforecasted north wind at 13-18 mph with gusts up to 30 mph.\nNot really near any wind advisory, but it will feel more brisk\nthan you are used to for late Summer. Some LREF members suggest\na 30-40% chance of gusts up to 35-40 mph at times Saturday.\n\n\nTemperatures gradually warm back up through next week:\n\nFollowing these briefly cool temperatures, global ensemble\nmembers gradually build the ridge back into the area, raising\ntemperatures back to near normal through next week. Rain chances\nare also rather low (<30%), and will likely continue to diminish\nas latest global model trends have backed off on a closed low\nsolutions for early next week. Would imagine forecasts to drop\nlower as the time-lagged models exit the NBM forecast.\n\nMoving forward, the CPC has the whole CONUS in 33-70% chances\nfor above normal temperatures in the 6-14 day range (50-60%\nchance for our area). Therefore, expect normal August weather to\nreturn towards the end of next week.",
